(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608319600-itonami-has-its-own-identity-and-the-claude-oauth-patch-is-upstream")
  (:adr/title "itonami is an app with its own identity on the hermes runtime, and the Claude OAuth patch it would have carried is already upstream")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-31")

  (:adr/context
  "Asked to design an `itonami-claude-auth` plugin for the itonami CLI,
   mirroring `kristianvast/hermes-claude-auth` (a Claude Code OAuth bypass for
   hermes-agent).

   The first version of this ADR read `~/.local/bin/itonami` — one line,
   `exec .../hermes -p itonami` — and concluded that itonami *is* a hermes
   profile, therefore not a separate thing to name. The owner corrected it the
   same day: hermes and itonami are different applications. The correction is
   right and the error is worth naming, because it is a shape that recurs.

   ONE PROCESS IS NOT ONE APPLICATION. Two boundaries were collapsed:

     runtime  — hermes-agent. One binary, one venv, one site-packages, shared
                by 25 profiles. Genuinely shared, and nothing per-app can be
                built at this layer.
     app      — itonami. Its own identity `did:webvh:...:itonami.cloud`, its
                own public face (itonami.cloud, @itonami), its own SOUL.md and
                scope, its own config.yaml / .env / state.db / skills / cron
                under `profiles/itonami/`.

   The per-app credential boundary is first-class, not incidental:
   `hermes_cli/env_loader.py` loads a profile's `.env` with `override=True` and
   then REMOVES profile-managed keys absent from it, so one app's credentials
   do not leak into another's runtime. An app here can hold its own key.

   What the shim proves is only that the two apps share an interpreter. That
   bears on whether an interpreter-level `.pth` patch can be per-app (it
   cannot). It says nothing about identity, which is what an auth plugin is
   actually about.")

  (:adr/decision
  "1. Do not port hermes-claude-auth under any name. Not because itonami is not
      a separate app — it is — but because the patch has no work left to do.
      Measured with no patch installed, a one-shot call reached
      api.anthropic.com and answered, on the Claude Code OAuth token. hermes
      0.20.6 ships the transforms first-party. See :adr/evidence.

   2. If a repo is later warranted, the name is `cloud-itonami/plugin-claude-auth`.
      Derivation: `plugin-` (ADR-2608318100) names the agent host being adapted;
      the host for this subject is the itonami app, and the org segment
      cloud-itonami already says itonami (ADR-2608040170), so the name carries
      only what is left — `plugin-claude-auth`. NOT `itonami-claude-auth`, which
      says itonami twice.

   3. Do not flip the itonami default to anthropic by editing
      `profiles/itonami/config.yaml`. That edit would be silently reverted:
      `scripts/hermes-hyakka-bots/resolve_free_model.cljs` owns the `model:`
      block and its `install-profile-configs!` writes it into EVERY profile's
      config.yaml — measured 2026-08-31 across 23 profiles. A change there is a
      change to the free-model floor (`free-model-policy.edn`), which has an
      owner, and belongs in that mechanism rather than beside it.")

  (:adr/evidence
  "PATCH NOT NEEDED — measured with `~/.hermes/patches/` absent and no `.pth`:

     $ itonami chat --provider anthropic -m claude-sonnet-4-6 -q 'Reply with exactly: OK' -Q
     OK

   agent.log for that turn:
     Lazy-installing anthropic==0.87.0 for feature 'provider.anthropic'
     provider=anthropic ... at https://api.anthropic.com
     API call #1: model=claude-sonnet-4-6 provider=anthropic in=15814 out=4 latency=4.1s

   The lazy install shows it was the first real Anthropic call on this machine,
   so no earlier state served it.

   The credential used was the Claude Code OAuth token and could have been
   nothing else:
     - read_claude_code_credentials() -> source=macos_keychain, sk-ant-oat
       (OAuth, not sk-ant-api), refresh present, valid. Read by known
       identifier, not by enumerating the keychain.
     - No ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in session env, in either .env, or in either
       config.yaml. The one injected secret is OPENROUTER_API_KEY from keychain
       item gftd.openrouter.
     - `hermes auth status anthropic` reports logged out; credential_pool holds
       only openrouter and copilot; active_provider is nil.

   WHY hermes no longer needs the patch — agent/anthropic_adapter.py:
     - build_anthropic_kwargs(..., is_oauth) docstring: 'When is_oauth is True,
       applies Claude Code compatibility transforms: system prompt prefix, tool
       name prefixing, and prompt sanitization.'
     - `oauth-2025-04-20` is in the beta header list at line 480.
   These are the transforms hermes-claude-auth existed to inject.

   NO AUTO-DISCOVERY — the item the first version of this ADR left UNVERIFIED
   is now measured, under HERMES_PROFILE=itonami:
     is_provider_explicitly_configured('anthropic')  = False
     is_provider_explicitly_configured('openrouter-free') = True
     _anthropic_oauth_credentials_present()          = True
   The credentials are present AND the gate refuses them. Anthropic is reached
   only on an explicit choice — the `--provider anthropic` flag in the smoke
   test was that choice. Claude is not silently reachable from itonami.")

  (:adr/consequences
  "Nothing was built and nothing was configured. Claude is reachable from
   itonami today with `itonami --provider anthropic -m <model>`, per invocation.

   The load-bearing output is negative: an agent asked for `itonami-claude-auth`
   should read this rather than clone a patch that would collide with hermes
   0.20.6's own OAuth path.

   WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and it is the part the correction exposes. On the
   `--provider anthropic` path, itonami borrows the OWNER's Claude Code session
   out of the macOS keychain. For an app that has its own DID and its own public
   face, that is the wrong shape: its usage is indistinguishable from the
   owner's, cannot be separately budgeted, revoked, or audited, and ends when
   the owner logs out. Giving itonami a credential of its own is a real design
   question with a real seam to build on (per-profile .env with override and
   leak removal). It is not the question that was asked here and is not decided
   by this ADR.

   Two things reopen the patch question, both measurable rather than assumed:
   a hermes update that drops the is_oauth transforms or the oauth-2025-04-20
   beta (re-run the smoke test; a 4xx from api.anthropic.com is the signal, not
   a fallback answering), and a decision to move the free-model floor.")

  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608318100-plugin-prefix-agent-host-plugin-adapter"
                "adr-2608040100-repo-name-four-planes"
                "adr-2608040170-identity-is-org-slash-name"))))
